  "facts": [
    "The Washington Bridge, opened in 1888, was the first U.S. bridge whose arch ribs were constructed from riveted plate girders, a pioneering design that set a new standard for steel arch construction.",
    "Each of the bridge’s two 510‑foot steel arches rises 134 feet above mean high water, providing a dramatic, 41‑meter vertical clearance that still accommodates modern river traffic.",
    "Designed by Charles C. Schneider and Wilhelm Hildenbrand, the Washington Bridge’s masonry approaches feature seven semicircular arches clad in granite and gneiss ashlar, a striking blend of stone and steel."
